package Patterns;

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Q2 {
    public static void main(String[] args){
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Please enter n: ");
        int n = input.nextInt();
        for (int i = 1; i <=n ; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <=i; j++) {
                System.out.print("* ");

            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}

OUTPUT:
n = 5
*
* *
* * *
* * * * 
* * * * *


Approach
Take an integer n as input from the user.
Use an outer loop to iterate from 1 to n, where each iteration represents a row.
For every row i, use an inner loop to print exactly i stars.
After printing the stars for the current row, move to the next line.
Continue until all n rows are printed.

Logic
The outer loop controls the number of rows.
The inner loop prints stars equal to the current row number.
As the row number increases, the number of stars increases by one, creating a right-angled triangular pattern.

Time Complexity
Outer loop runs n times.
Inner loop runs 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n times.

Total operations:

n(n + 1) / 2

Time Complexity: O(n²)

Space Complexity

Space Complexity: O(1)
